\details{
\strong{The model.} In functional coding the genotypic value is
\verb{g = sum_j a_j (x_j - 1) + sum_j d_j 1[x_j = 1] + sum_(k,l) e_kl (x_k - 1)(x_l - 1)}, \code{x} the dosage of allele 1. Its exact
statistical (NOIA) re-expression has additive effects
\verb{alpha_j = a_j + b_j d_j + sum_l e_jl c_l} (\verb{c_l = 2 p_l - 1}), dominance
effects \code{d} and A x A effects \code{e}. The three targets are covariances of
those three components.

\strong{When the result is exact.} Every present block (\code{G_A}, and \code{G_D} /
\code{G_AA} when present) is delivered under the named anchor to a relative
tolerance of \code{1e-8} on the correlation scale. That is checked, never
assumed: a calibration that misses it is an error before anything is
written.

\strong{How.} Highest order first: the A x A architecture is calibrated to
\code{G_AA}; the dominance architecture, \verb{d = h |a|} with dominance degrees
\code{h ~ N(dominance_degree_mean, dominance_degree_sd)}, is calibrated to
\code{G_D}; then, with the coupling \verb{b d + sum e c} the first two induce now
fixed, the additive architecture is solved so the statistical additive
effects deliver \code{G_A}. Each stage is a \verb{k x k} right factor of its drawn
architecture, so the same seed gives the same architecture.

\strong{The additive floor.} Dominance and epistasis already induce additive
variance (\verb{b d} and \verb{e c} are part of \code{alpha}). Part of it the additive
architecture can cancel, part it cannot; \code{G_A} below what it cannot cancel
is refused, and the error names that minimum per trait. The floor belongs
to the \strong{sampled architecture}, not to \code{G_D} / \code{G_AA} alone: another seed
gives another floor, and with every base frequency at \code{0.5} under
\code{"genic"} it is zero however large \code{G_D} is.

\strong{The anchor} is the reference covariance the calibration is exact for:
\itemize{
\item \code{"genic"} (default): the random-mating (HWE + linkage-equilibrium) limit
at the base allele frequencies. Use it for multi-generation studies.
\item \code{"realised"}: the covariances of the individuals \code{base_tbl} selects,
linkage disequilibrium included. The effects are still \strong{stored} with
HWE-referenced contrasts centred at the base frequencies, so the stored
\code{additive} / \code{dominance} split is not the cohort's; the total is exact,
and \code{\link[=extract_genetic_variance]{extract_genetic_variance()}}\code{(anchor = "realised")} on the same
individuals gives the targets back. \code{base_tbl} must select individuals
with complete genotypes, and the in-memory design arrays have a size
limit.
}

Under linkage disequilibrium the \code{additive} component of a realised cohort
is a contrast component, not a joint regression: calibration is exact for
the anchor and nothing more.

After calibration the delivered blocks are compared with what another
population sees: the genic limit under \code{"realised"}; the base individuals'
own covariances under \code{"genic"} when \code{base_tbl} selects individuals; the
founder pool's expectation of the additive block only (dominance and A x A
would need the pool's multi-locus LD). A departure outside \code{warn_bounds}
warns (the pool comparison is a message). Nothing is stored.
}
\section{Targets}{

Each block (\code{additive}, \code{dominance}, \code{additive_by_additive}) comes from
exactly one source:
\itemize{
\item a passed matrix (\code{G_A}, \code{G_D}, \code{G_AA}; a number for one trait), written
to \code{trait_var_comp} in the same transaction as the effects. It is refused
when that block is already stored for any of the traits, even as an
identical matrix, and whether or not \code{trait_var_comp_tbl} shows it;
\item otherwise the rows of \code{trait_var_comp_tbl} (a filtered
\code{get_table(pop, "trait_var_comp")}), or, when it is \code{NULL}, the stored
population-wide rows.
}

A block that comes from neither is \strong{absent} from the model; a zero matrix
is an explicit exact zero, and writes no terms. An \code{additive} block is
required. A model with no non-zero dominance or A x A block takes the
additive-only route: the same draw, calibration and rows as
\code{\link[=define_additive_effects]{define_additive_effects()}}. To add a block later, store or pass it and
re-run: the additive architecture draw is unchanged by the new block (same
seed).

Non-zero dominance or A x A targets need a positive-definite \code{G_A}: this
release solves the additive stage for a full-rank factor. A correlation of
+/-1 or a zero additive variance is accepted for additive-only models.
Singular targets are reported with a message, since a typed \code{1} meant as
\code{0.99} looks the same.
}

\section{Pairs}{

A x A acts on pairs of QTL, used only with an \code{additive_by_additive} block:
\itemize{
\item \code{pairs}, a data frame \code{locus_name_1}, \code{locus_name_2}: your design. A
locus may appear in several pairs (a hub gene); unknown loci, loci
outside the filter, self-pairs and repeated pairs (in either order) are
errors.
\item \code{pairs = NULL}: a random matching, each QTL in at most one pair, as
AlphaSimR does. \code{n_pairs = NULL} pairs every QTL once
(\code{floor(m / 2)} pairs), and a message says so.
}

Within a pair the loci are ordered by name (C locale), and pairs are
written sorted by locus id, so the stored order does not depend on the
draw.
}

\section{Crossbreeding and scope}{

Effects are common to every line (no \code{line_name} or \code{parent_origin}; line
or parent-specific non-additive effects are a later release). Common
effects still give heterosis, through allele-frequency differences between
lines. The default base pools the founder table, so the targets then hold
at the \strong{pooled} frequencies (with a Wahlund warning); pass \code{base_tbl} to
calibrate on one reference line.
}

\section{What a re-run replaces}{

Every \code{"generated"} term of the traits, at every scope: line-scoped
additive variants from \code{\link[=define_additive_effects]{define_additive_effects()}} included (the message
counts them). Terms of other owners are untouched. Afterwards
\code{\link[=define_additive_effects]{define_additive_effects()}} refuses these traits while their generated
model has dominance or A x A terms; re-run this function with an
additive-only target, or use \code{\link[=remove_generated_effects]{remove_generated_effects()}}.
}

\section{Generated means calibrated}{

There is no option to write fixed or unscaled effects. Exact coefficients
go through \code{\link[=define_genome_effect_terms]{define_genome_effect_terms()}} with \code{\link[=ad_terms]{ad_terms()}} /
\code{\link[=aa_terms]{aa_terms()}} under a user owner, for example:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{pop |>
  define_genome_effect_terms(
    trait_name = "ADG",
    terms = rbind(
      ad_terms(locus_name = c("Locus_10", "Locus_44"),
               a = c(0.30, -0.12), d = c(0.10, 0.05), p = c(0.35, 0.60),
               coding = "functional"),
      aa_terms(locus_name_1 = "Locus_10", locus_name_2 = "Locus_44",
               e = 0.08, p_1 = 0.35, p_2 = 0.60, coding = "functional")))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Inbreeding depression}{

\code{inbreeding_depression} (the drop in the mean per unit of inbreeding \code{F},
positive = depression, \verb{sum 2pq d}) sets each named trait's mean
dominance degree. It is exact for one trait. With several traits each
requested trait's mean is solved as if it stood alone, and the joint
calibration then mixes the columns, so the delivered depression is
approximate, with no closeness guarantee; a message gives requested and
delivered values. It is not stored.
}

